SECTION 4. WHAT THIS LICENSE DOES NOT PROHIBIT
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

Subject to full compliance with Section 3, the following
activities are expressly permitted:

4.1  A commercial organization may charge for a product,
     platform, service, subscription, or application that
     includes, embeds, or provides access to Pythos Functionality,
     provided the Operator satisfies all Free Access Preservation
     requirements of Section 3.

4.2  An Operator may charge for:

     (a) installation, configuration, or deployment services;
     (b) technical support, consulting, or training;
     (c) custom development or integration work;
     (d) infrastructure, hosting, or server management for a
         customer's own Pythos deployment;
     (e) proprietary features, content, curriculum, analytics,
         or other value genuinely separate from and independent
         of Pythos Functionality, provided Pythos Functionality
         remains accessible under Section 3; or
     (f) enhanced AI models, improved performance, additional
         academic subjects, or other genuine improvements beyond
         the Official Free Pythos Service.

4.3  An Operator may operate an advertising-supported service
     providing Pythos Functionality without violating this
     license, provided Users are not charged money for access.

4.4  An Operator may accept voluntary donations, grants, or
     sponsorships related to Pythos development or deployment,
     provided access to Pythos Functionality is not conditioned
     on such contributions.

4.5  A paid product may limit access to its own proprietary
     infrastructure, features, and capabilities to paying
     customers. The obligation under Section 3 is not to make
     the paid product free — it is to preserve the path to the
     Official Free Pythos Service for every User.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 5. THE LINE: PRODUCT VERSUS ACCESS
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

5.1  PERMITTED.

     A company may say:
     "Our $20/month platform includes Pythos."

     provided it also clearly tells Users:
     "Pythos is available free at https://pythos.lanzar.me/"

     and that link genuinely works without payment.

5.2  PROHIBITED.

     A company may NOT:

     (a) tell Users: "Our $20/month platform includes Pythos —
         there is no free access to Pythos"; or

     (b) make Pythos Functionality accessible only to paying
         customers while concealing or failing to disclose the
         free alternative.

5.3  DIRECT CHARGE FOR PYTHOS AS A SERVICE.

     Where an Operator's sole or dominant commercial purpose —
     as evidenced by the Operator's own marketing materials,
     product description, or user interface — is to charge Users
     for access to Pythos Functionality, the Free Access Link
     requirement does NOT make such an arrangement compliant.
     Providing a link to the free service does not cure a
     violation of this section, because Users are paying for
     access to Pythos Functionality directly, not for a
     surrounding product in which Pythos is incidentally included.

     The distinction is:
     - Paying for a product that INCLUDES Pythos, with free
       access preserved → permitted under Section 4.
     - Paying for ACCESS TO PYTHOS ITSELF → prohibited, even
       if a free link is provided.

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
SECTION 6. NETWORK SERVICES AND SOURCE AVAILABILITY
━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

6.1  If you operate a Pythos Network Service using a Covered
     Work, you must make the Complete Corresponding Source Code
     of that Covered Work available to Users at no charge, by:

     (a) providing a prominent link to a publicly accessible
         repository containing the Complete Corresponding
         Source Code; or

     (b) offering in writing to provide the Complete Corresponding
         Source Code on request.

6.2  Source availability under this section does not satisfy
     the Free Access Preservation requirements of Section 3.
     Both obligations apply independently.

